<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Tucumán</h1><p class="st-meta">1820 CE – 1821 CE · capital San Miguel de Tucumán</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived provincial republic in northwestern Argentina created by the caudillo Bernabé Aráoz during the anarchy of 1820.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After central government in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata</a> collapsed in 1820, the governor of Tucumán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabe-araoz">Bernabé Aráoz</a>, proclaimed the Republic of Tucumán. It claimed the provinces of Tucumán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catamarca-province">Catamarca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-del-estero">Santiago del Estero</a>, and adopted a constitution that year.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabe-araoz">Aráoz</a> governed as supreme president, seeking to keep the northern provinces together under Tucumán's leadership. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-del-estero">Santiago del Estero</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-felipe-ibarra">Juan Felipe Ibarra</a>, rejected his authority and declared its own autonomy, and the two sides went to war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catamarca-province">Catamarca</a> also broke away. The republic faced constant conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a>, governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-miguel-de-guemes">Martín Miguel de Güemes</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabe-araoz">Aráoz</a> was overthrown in 1821, and the republic dissolved into the separate provinces of Tucumán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catamarca-province">Catamarca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-del-estero">Santiago del Estero</a>, which later joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argentine-confederation">Argentine Confederation</a>. Aráoz was executed in 1824.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-tucuman.jpg" alt="Republic of Tucumán" width="320" height="207"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_Tucuman.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1820 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>San Miguel de Tucumán</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bernabé Aráoz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>República del Tucumán</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1820&amp;focus=republic-of-tucuman" aria-label="Republic of Tucumán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2962 3162 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Tucumán lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3282 3418L3517 3686"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3517 3686h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3282 3418h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3282 3418h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks San Miguel de Tucumán. <a href="/atlas?year=1820&amp;focus=republic-of-tucuman">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
